A formatter supplies a String receiving context. A formatter on a construction
with a known sequence receiver is a type conflict. Choose one formatter per
head. Each formatter applies to its own body. A nested child chooses its own
formatting. In a `.mtf` file the outer body and nested templates without an
explicit directive default to `$md`. Any explicit nested directive overrides
that default. See @../moth-templates (Moth template files).

### Whitespace and Markdown

`$raw` takes no operand. It keeps authored body whitespace, including leading
and trailing spaces and line breaks. Nested templates and expressions still
contribute their values. `$md` also takes no operand. It formats Moth's
Markdown, which supports explicit section IDs and treats child templates as
opaque formatting boundaries.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    details = [$md:Current status: [$html:<em>ready</em>].]
]

The outer template supplies a paragraph. The child supplies its own HTML.

### HTML, escaped text and CSS

`$html` takes no operand. It keeps authored markup and can warn about constructs
such as a script tag or inline event handler. `$escape_html` also takes no
operand. It replaces ampersands, angle brackets and both kinds of quotes in
authored text with HTML entities. Nested templates and dynamic expressions
pass through as separate contributions. A surrounding escape directive cannot
escape a child's output after the child has rendered.

`$css` checks CSS block syntax. The optional compile-time String operand
`"inline"` selects declaration syntax. The checker can warn about malformed
CSS. The resulting String becomes page content only when the application
uses it. Supply a tracked CSS file to the entry's `stylesheets` destination
to share rules across a page.

### Code listings

`$code` takes an optional compile-time String naming a supported language.
Omit it for generic highlighting, use `"moth"` for Moth source or use
`"text"` to escape HTML-sensitive characters without adding syntax classes.
The code body keeps balanced brackets as source instead of interpreting
them as nested templates. For a multiline listing the first content line
sets the indentation baseline. Deeper lines keep their relative indentation.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    example = [$code "moth":
        greeting = [:
            Hello, [name]
        ]
    ]
]

The listing shows `name` as written, without looking up its value. A formatter
controls text and composition. It cannot rewrite functions, change a nominal
type or change language safety rules.
